WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.920
SPEAKER 1: Hi, folks, and
welcome to the session

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.070
on advanced IAM, where we'll
be covering tips and tricks

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.530
around IAM policy management.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.870
I'm Blake, a product
manager here at Google

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.450
where I primarily focus on
the Cloud Identity and Access

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:20.690
Management product, which serves
as a central point for access

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.790
policy management
and authorization

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.180
enforcement across the
Google Cloud platform.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.430
We're here today to dive
a bit deeper than usual

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.660
into how you manage
your access policies,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.689
particularly through
the API, as well

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:35.270
as best practices in this area.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.730
We're going to be taking a look
at the policies themselves,

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.680
where they can be
configured across your cloud

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.290
infrastructure, how,
based on your policies,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:45.290
Google determines when to
grant access to a user,

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.120
different versions
of the policy schema

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.850
that are available, and
finally, how you can manage

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.740
your policies more
safely by following

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.400
some simple best practices.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.072
Let's look at how IAM
policies appear themselves

00:00:57.072 --> 00:00:59.280
when you're managing them
through the G Cloud command

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.550
line or directly
through the API.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.010
Amongst some other aspects
that we'll get into in a bit,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.230
they're primarily
made up of bindings.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.630
In this example, you can
see the first binding grants

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:13.050
the storage admin role to
an individual user, Alex,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.120
as well as to a
group called Admins.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.180
There's a second
binding in this policy

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.000
that grants Bob the
compute admin role.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.720
Roles, of course, are groups
of granular permissions in GCP.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.220
The storage admin
role would let you

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.030
create buckets and retrieve or
store objects in those buckets.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.620
The compute admin
role would contain

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.683
the permissions to do things
like create VM instances.

00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:37.600
So you may have noticed
that the policies only

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.580
talk about who can do what.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.380
They didn't indicate the where--

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.960
in effect, in what areas of
your cloud infrastructure

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.710
they can perform these actions.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.720
That's where policy
attachment comes in.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.450
Your cloud infrastructure
in GCP is organized

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.040
in the resource hierarchy.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.320
All of it exists under
the Organization node,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.200
which typically represents
the company you work for.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.910
Under this, you
can create folders

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.090
for further categorization,
such as business units, regions,

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.770
or environment types.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.660
Ultimately, though, your
cloud resources themselves,

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.009
such as the storage buckets,
VMs, or database instances,

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:14.640
will end up being created
and managed in Projects.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.340
Now IAM policies can be
attached to any of these nodes.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.300
The higher they're
placed in the hierarchy,

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.510
the more they grant access to.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:26.150
This is because the effects
of policies inherent down.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.970
Because projects usually end
up being a logical grouping

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.020
of resources that represent
a particular workload,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.940
they're a frequent attachment
point for IAM policies

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.070
to keep the effect of
those policies scoped

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.560
to just that workload.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.540
However, for many
resource types,

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:44.210
it is possible to set
the policy even lower.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.820
You could, for example,
grant a user access just

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:51.250
to a single VM in a project
rather than all of them.

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.250
So let's take a look at
how you can set policies

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.260
at different levels in GCP.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.360
The policies are
retrieved or modified

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.920
through APIs that
are made available

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.220
by the service related to where
you want to set the policy.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.130
For example, setting policies
and projects, folders,

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.860
or the Organization node is
all done through the resource

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.630
manager service.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.780
Or setting a policy on a
particular Pub/Sub topic

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.790
would be done through
the Pub/Sub service.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.280
The method to
modify IAM policies

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.530
is called setIamPolicy.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.930
Here, you can see it exposed
on the Resource Manager

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.500
endpoint for projects or
the Pub/Sub endpoint related

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.650
to topics.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.950
But how can you find which
resource types support

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.670
IAM policy attachment and
which APIs to call for each?

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.618
One good place to start is the
API reference documentation.

00:03:40.618 --> 00:03:42.660
Start by looking up the
reference for the service

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.900
related to where you
want to set a policy.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.240
Next, find the resource
type upon which

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.670
you wish to set that policy.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.960
And lastly, look for
the setIamPolicy method.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.580
Note too, where
this exists, there

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.380
will also be a
getIamPolicy method,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.010
which is what you can use to
retrieve any existing policy

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.580
attached to that resource.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.290
All of this documentation
is available

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:09.000
on cloud.google.com/docs.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.010
But what if you want
to get a bit fancier?

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.230
Google provides an
API discovery service

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.260
which itself is an API you
can use to pull information

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.720
about APIs that Google
makes available, including

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.640
the methods they provide.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.770
Let's take a look at
the listApis call.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:25.810
As you probably
expected from its name,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.420
it returns a list of
Google APIs you can use.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.230
In the example
here, we're looking

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.513
at the compute API entry that
was returned from this call.

00:04:34.513 --> 00:04:36.930
This, of course, refers to the
Google Cloud Compute Engine

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:39.000
API.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.180
In its listing, it also
includes the discovery rest URL

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.490
for the service.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.580
Let's query that.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.710
In reviewing the discovery
document for a specific API,

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.080
you can see all resource
types it exposes

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:55.570
and the methods supported on
each of those resource types.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:57.680
So for example, we can
see here that there

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.057
is a setIamPolicy method
available for OS images,

00:05:01.057 --> 00:05:02.640
meaning that you can
set different IAM

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.150
policies on different
OS images if you wish.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.160
For you to set the
right policies,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.440
it's helpful to understand
how Google evaluates them

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.050
when making access decisions.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:16.590
Referring back to our
resource hierarchy,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.060
let's say Alice wants to
delete a particular bucket,

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.390
called bucket A,
inside of this project.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.850
This is the IAM policy
set on that project.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:28.730
In it, we can see a binding that
grants Alice the storage admin

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:31.080
role.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.480
So to IAM, what are
our inputs here?

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.820
To evaluate, we effectively
need to determine

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.300
if a certain principle, Alice,
has the storage buckets delete

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:44.800
permission to be used
against bucket A. To do so,

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.870
we need to grab any
potentially relevant IAM

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.265
policies in the hierarchy.

00:05:49.265 --> 00:05:50.640
This includes
grabbing the policy

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.160
on the bucket itself,
the project it's in,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.050
the folder that
project is under,

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.015
and the policy on the
organization node itself.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.330
So we have our query, and we
have all the relevant policies

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:06.140
here.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:09.200
Evaluation ultimately
happens binding by binding.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.120
The default state is
not to grant access

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.920
unless a policy
explicitly says to.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.500
Starting at the bucket, we
can see its policy contains

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:18.580
no bindings.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.890
So this policy does
not grant access.

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.740
In moving up to the
project, however,

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.620
we see our binding from before
that grants Alice the storage

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:27.820
admin role.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.310
Because Alice is
indicated in the policy

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.680
and the storage admin
role contains the storage

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.650
buckets delete permission, this
binding would grant access.

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.610
In continuing, however, we
encounter another binding.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.680
Alice is not listed
in this binding,

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:47.320
nor does the computer admin role
contain the needed permission.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.780
At the folder level,
we see that group Foo

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:51.070
is granted the owner role.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.200
But because Alice is
not in that group,

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:57.087
this binding also does
not grant her access.

00:06:57.087 --> 00:06:59.670
This is similar when looking at
the policy on the organization

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:00.390
node.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.970
Alice is neither a member
of the Admins group,

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.680
nor is she a member
of the security group.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.750
All that said, the
evaluation decision

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:10.720
here is to allow her
to delete the bucket.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.510
This is because all IAM
bindings are additive.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.090
If any grant access, the
action will be allowed.

00:07:18.555 --> 00:07:20.930
Especially when you have a
large amount of infrastructure

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:22.970
deployed to Google
Cloud, it can sometimes

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:26.000
be difficult to determine
whether a user does or does not

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.020
have access to perform a given
action on a specific resource.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.690
To help you better understand
and visualize the evaluation

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:34.490
logic we just went
through, Google

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:36.990
provides a tool in the
cloud console web UI called

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.780
the policy troubleshooter.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.750
In it, you can enter exactly
the same three pieces of input

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.190
we just talked about--

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:47.070
the principle, the
permission, and the resource.

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.770
Policy troubleshooter will then
show you the effective access

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:53.070
decision as well as all the
bindings and their effects

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.350
in each relevant IAM policy.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.390
This can help you make
adjustments to policies

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:02.450
if the resulting access decision
is not what you intended.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.510
To recap, where you
attach policies matters.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.078
You can simplify
policy management

00:08:07.078 --> 00:08:08.870
by setting them further
up in the hierarchy

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:12.380
to grant more access but also
lower down on the project

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.780
or on individual
resources when access

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.000
needs to be more fine grained.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.740
All bindings are
additive, so it's

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.990
important to understand the
bindings you've configured

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.870
to ensure you're not granting
access to someone you did not

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.770
intend.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.510
Understanding the permissions
in the roles you're granting

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:30.510
and who are members of
the groups to whom you've

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:32.490
granted access as
well as, of course,

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.530
who controls the
membership in those groups,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.080
are both important aspects
of making informed policy

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.950
management decisions.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.950
And finally, you can
use tools that Google

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.169
provides to better understand
the effective state

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:43.877
of your policies.

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.060
One more thing before we move
on just because sometimes this

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:50.387
trips people up--

00:08:50.387 --> 00:08:51.970
for some types of
permissions, they're

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:53.770
checked relative
to the parent node

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.440
compared to where you might
think that they would be.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.200
Let's take the example of Alice
trying to create a bucket.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.050
Here, we see she's exercising
the storage buckets create

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:04.100
permission.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.520
But the resource upon which
this check is being performed

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:11.600
is the project in which she's
trying to create the bucket.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.430
These parent checks
occur on permissions

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:15.620
related to creating
new resources

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:18.980
as well as listing resources
within a certain node.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.230
So particularly for
list permissions,

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.810
make sure you're granted
these on the parent container

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.970
if you wish for a user
to be able to enumerate

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:31.130
all resources of a certain
type within that container.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:33.860
Moving on, IAM has
added some new features

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:35.180
since it first launched.

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:36.980
Recently, one such
feature required

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:41.180
us to version the schema of
the policy content itself.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:44.170
Let's take a look at a
basic policy example.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.850
In February of this
year, though, we

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:48.460
introduced a feature called
IAM conditions, which

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:52.590
allows you to configure when
bindings should apply or not.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:55.370
Here, you can see a condition
was added to the binding that

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.860
states its effect is
only valid for requests

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:02.487
made before January 1 of 2021.

00:10:02.487 --> 00:10:04.320
But you might have also
noticed the presence

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:06.960
of this condition bumped the
version field in the policy

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.520
from 1 to 3.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.990
When IAM originally
launched, there

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.750
were two assumptions baked
into the policy schema.

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:15.640
One was that each
finding included

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:19.630
one role and a list of members,
and in any given policy,

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:22.600
there would only be one
binding for a given role.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.760
Conditions fundamentally
changed this assumption.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:27.770
Now you could have two bindings
for the same role granted

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:31.560
to different people under
different conditions.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.740
This has an effect on
how you retrieve policies

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:35.630
as well as how you modify them.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:38.540
When retrieving a policy
using getIamPolicy,

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:41.730
you can specify which schema
version you're requesting.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:44.450
If you request version three,
it will return the policy

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:46.820
with the conditions
objects included.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.860
If, however, you
specify version one

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:51.470
or do not specify a
version, you will not

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:54.020
receive all the details
of the conditions.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:56.690
Similarly for
setIamPolicy, if you're

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:59.390
submitting a policy that
includes conditions objects,

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:03.140
you must set its
version field to three.

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.780
Taking a look here, if
you ran getIamPolicy

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.425
with the requested policy
version set to three

00:11:08.425 --> 00:11:09.800
you'll see the
same return we saw

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:12.960
earlier with the
conditioned object intact.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.210
However, schema version
one is requested

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.095
for a policy that
contains conditions,

00:11:17.095 --> 00:11:18.720
you'll only see an
indicator that there

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.840
is a condition on a binding
via a hash concatenated

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:24.550
with the granted role name.

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.800
In reality, though,
this is pretty simple.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:29.170
For existing and future
programmatic integrations

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.720
that need to read or
write IAM policies,

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:35.560
just ensure they understand the
syntax and semantics associated

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:37.510
with the version three schema.

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:39.580
Namely, this means your
code should understand

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.010
that there can be multiple
instances of the same roll

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:44.680
across bindings
in a given policy

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:47.810
and that some of those bindings
may include a condition.

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:49.410
So as long as this
is the case, you

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:52.480
can always request version
three on getIamPolicy

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.310
and set version three
on setIamPolicy.

00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:59.550
Finally today, let's
talk about best practices

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:03.470
when it comes to safely
modifying IAM policies.

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:07.100
Critically here is understanding
the get modify set flow.

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:09.650
To modify any IAM
policy, you must first

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:12.880
retrieve the current
state of the policy.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:15.880
This is done by executing
a getIamPolicy call

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:20.010
against the resource to
which policy is attached.

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:23.010
Once retrieved, your code can
modify the policy in any way

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:25.000
necessary.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:27.850
For example, you may wish
to add an additional binding

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:28.600
to the policy--

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:33.190
in this case, giving Bob
the compute admin role.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:35.680
Once you or your code have
made the desired changes,

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:37.810
the entire policy
is submitted back

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.270
using the setIamPolicy call.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.940
In this example, that
includes any bindings

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:45.940
that were already there, along
with the additional binding

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:48.300
that you've added.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.130
A common mistake many people
make early on in using IAM

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:54.520
is only submitting their
changes in setIamPolicy

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.640
rather than the entire desired
state of the policy inclusive

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.170
of their changes.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:02.590
And any local modification
performed by you or your code

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:04.180
should follow the
rules associated

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:06.760
with the policy schema version
being requested or set.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.160
Another best
practice is ensuring

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:14.160
appropriate concurrency control
is in place for your policy

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:15.680
modifications.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:18.180
Let's take a look at an example
where Alice and Bob are both

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:22.400
trying to modify a policy
at roughly the same time.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:24.880
First, Alice
executes getIamPolicy

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:28.320
as the start of her get
modify set sequence.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:30.810
She or her code then
begin making any desired

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.290
modifications to
the policy locally.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:37.440
Bob then kicks off his get
modify set flow by requesting

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:43.670
the policy currently set and
begins his local modifications.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.550
Here is code then
complete their changes

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:49.420
and successfully set
the updated policy.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:52.450
Shortly after, however,
Alice's local modifications

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:56.310
are complete, and her
updated policy is set.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:58.680
This, of course, has a
very unexpected outcome.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.380
Because the basis on which
Alice's local modifications

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:04.800
were performed was the result
of a getIamPolicy issued

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:08.070
before Bob's setIamPolicy,
Bob's changes

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:12.800
were effectively overwritten
by Alice's setIamPolicy.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.620
To prevent situations like
this where two people may

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:18.110
accidentally step on
each other's changes,

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.530
IAM supports e-tags to
provide concurrency control.

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:23.420
When retrieving
an IAM policy, it

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:25.370
will include an e-tag
field that represents

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.940
the state of the policy
as it exists at that time.

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:31.280
During your modification,
be sure to preserve

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:35.540
this e-tag field exactly as it
was returned from getIamPolicy,

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:38.930
then ensure the same e-tag
is included in the payload

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.420
during your setIamPolicy.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:44.090
If the policy on that resource
has changed since you last

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:50.050
retrieved it, your setIamPolicy
call will appropriately fail.

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:53.020
So to recap, again, always
preserve the e-tag field

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:56.000
all the way through the
get modify set flow.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.020
If the policy on a
resource has changed,

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:01.300
your setIamPolicy call
will fail with an HTTP 409

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:02.720
conflict response.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:04.720
And in this case,
your client can simply

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:08.260
retry the entire get
modify set operation so as

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:10.720
to incorporate whatever the
latest state of the policy

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:11.670
is into its changes.

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:16.790
While not directly related
to the access management

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.220
focus of this
breakout, IAM policies

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:22.250
do contain a field related to
the audit logging configuration

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:24.200
called AuditConfigs.

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:27.230
When performing changes intended
to grant or revoke access,

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:29.690
just be sure not to
drop or otherwise modify

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:32.330
this field, as you may
inadvertently change your audit

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.520
logging configuration.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.400
Great, so let's
pull what we just

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:40.730
went through in this breakout
together into a single example.

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:42.560
Here, we have a
Python script intended

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:45.260
to add Alice to a
project's IAM policy,

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:48.510
granting her the
computer admin role.

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:51.180
Note that we first begin by
retrieving the policy as it

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.850
currently exists on
the project and doing

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.550
so with the requested
policy version of three

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:59.190
because our code complies
with all the rules defined

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:02.520
in the version three schema.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:06.990
We store that retrieved
policy locally.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.270
Now moving on to the second
part of the Python script,

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:12.490
we can begin our
local modifications.

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.680
You'll know that we focus
our change specifically

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.470
to the binding section
of the retrieve policy,

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:20.080
simply appending Alice
and her new role grant

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.100
to the retrieved policy.

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:23.920
Keeping the changes
focused here safely

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:27.100
preserves the tag and
AuditConfigs fields

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:30.350
as originally retrieved.

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:32.360
Next, because we know
our code complies

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:35.390
with the semantics of the
policy three schema version,

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:38.600
we can go ahead and set the
policy version to three.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:40.400
While there weren't
any conditions included

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.773
on the new binding in
this specific example,

00:16:42.773 --> 00:16:44.690
your code will probably
need to accept changes

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:46.200
of many different types.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.590
Some of those may
include conditions.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:51.230
As a result, any IAM
clients you write

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:54.750
can always express version three
in their set IAM policy calls

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:56.360
so long as the version
three semantics

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:59.910
are always safely followed.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:02.130
Finally, we submit
the updated policy

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:07.230
to Google Cloud using
the SetIamPolicy call.

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:09.150
Last recap of the
breakout-- make

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.220
sure to preserve the e-tag
throughout the policy

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.990
modification process.

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:15.750
Only touch the fields
you need when modifying,

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:17.670
and make sure any
programmatic integrations

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:19.470
you develop follow
the assumptions

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:22.547
baked into the version
three policy schema.

00:17:22.547 --> 00:17:23.880
And that's all I have for today.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:25.589
I'd like to thank
everyone for watching.

00:17:25.589 --> 00:17:27.948
And if you can't wait to
get even more IAM info,

00:17:27.948 --> 00:17:29.490
we have a couple
other great sessions

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:31.800
dropping this week I
encourage you to check out

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:33.930
covering IAM
Recommender and Policy

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:36.180
Intelligence, both
of which can be

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:37.970
invaluable in helping
you achieve least

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:39.730
privilege in your environments.

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:40.430
Thanks again.

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.260
Have a great day.

